for.\n```\n\nSo the reason it's gnome-excluded is that gnome runs already score better from deep dives, and we need to keep them byte-identical to the parent. But our goal is to IMPROVE the score, so we should modify this.\n\nLet me make the change. I'll remove the gnome exclusion from `proactive_sleep_strategy`.\n\nActually, let me think more carefully. The comment says \"the gnome runs' whole score comes from a few RNG-fragile deep dives that must stay byte-identical to the parent.\" This means the gnome runs are already fragile, and changing them might break the deep runs that give them their score. But the whole point of this exercise IS to improve the score, so we need to make changes that may break byte-identical runs.\n\nOK, I'll proceed with removing the gnome exclusion. But let me also think about whether there are other changes that might be more targeted.\n\nActually, looking at this more carefully, I realize there might be a subtler issue. The `proactive_sleep_strategy` is preempted in the strategy chain. If I remove the gnome exclusion, the gnome Healer will try to zap sleeping monsters before engaging in melee. This should help survival, but it might also change the game in ways that don't always improve the score.\n\nLet me also consider: maybe instead of modifying `proactive_sleep_strategy`, I should look at what's happening in the early game more carefully. The bot starts with a scalpel, and the first monsters it encounters (gnome zombie, cave spider, etc.) are potentially deadly. If the bot could just survive the first few encounters, it would get a weapon and start progressing.\n\nLet me make the change and see what happens. I'll remove the gnome check from `proactive_sleep_strategy`.\n\nWait, I should also consider: is the wand of sleep even available early enough? Let me check what items the gnome Healer starts with and what's typically found early.\n\nThe Healer starts with:\n- A scalpel (weapon)\n- A stethoscope (tool)\n- Some food\n- Possibly