{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790541337003,"sessionID":"ses_f1b882f77ffebyKXRbssgjp7gn","part":{"id":"prt_0e4943e42001BMerAPFOsjFTB7","messageID":"msg_0e49439ba001Gi3NcyCdMS7IQn","sessionID":"ses_f1b882f77ffebyKXRbssgjp7gn","type":"reasoning","text":"Let me look at the full `open_visit_search` function and how it decides what to explore. The priority map (`prio`) is key - it determines which tiles the bot wants to visit. \n\nLooking at the priority calculations:\n1. `prio -= level.search_count ** 2 * 2` - tiles that have been searched a lot get lower priority\n2. `prio += (is_on_door & (stones > 3)) * 250` - tiles next to doors with many stones nearby get higher priority\n3. `prio += (np.stack([...]).sum(0) <= 1) * 250` - tiles with <=1 walkable neighbor get higher priority (corridors)\n4. `prio[(stones == 0) & (walls == 0)] = -np.inf` - tiles with no stones and no walls get -inf priority (empty floor?)\n5. `prio[~level.walkable | (dis == -1)] = -np.inf` - non-walkable or unreachable tiles get -inf\n\nThe priority seems to favor corridors and areas near doors/stones. But I'm not seeing an obvious issue that would cause early deaths.\n\nLet me try a completely different approach. Let me look at what specific code change could help, by examining the relationship between the successful seeds and the rest.\n\nActually, let me try something simpler. What if I modify the `proactive_sleep_strategy` to include gnome Healers but with a more conservative approach - only enabling it when the bot has a wand of sleep AND is at low HP? This might avoid the negative effects I saw earlier.\n\nWait, actually, let me re-read the previous result where I enabled proactive sleep for gnomes. The mean went from 0.112 to 0.099, with seed 8 going from 0.554 to 0.365. The issue was that seed 8 got worse. But seeds 7 stayed the same (0.466). And most other seeds didn't change much.\n\nActually, looking at the data from that run:\n- Seed 7: 0.466 -> 0.466 (same)\n- Seed 8: 0.554 -> 0.365 (worse)\n- Seed 1: 0.051 -> 0.051 (same)\n- Seed 11: 0.161 -> 0.161 (same)\n- Most other seeds: same or very similar\n\nSo only seed 8 was negatively affected. This might be because seed 8's particular dungeon layout interacts poorly with the proactive sleep strategy. Maybe the wand of sleep is being used on a monster that the bot would have survived anyway, or the wizard is using charges on a less important target.\n\nLet me try the proactive sleep change again, but this time I'll add a safeguard: only enable it when the bot has a wand of sleep AND has been playing for enough turns (to avoid early-game situations where the wand isn't found yet).\n\nActually, the `proactive_sleep_strategy` already checks for a wand of sleep - if there's no wand, it yields False. So the change should only affect seeds where the gnome Healer finds a wand of sleep early.\n\nLet me think about why seed 8 got worse. Maybe the proactive sleep is using the wand on a monster that isn't actually threatening, or the wand zap is missing, or the monster wakes up too quickly.\n\nActually, you know what, let me just try the proactive sleep change again and see if the results are consistent. Maybe the first run was a fluke.\n\nBut before that, let me think of other approaches.\n\nWhat if I make the bot engrave Elbereth earlier? The `emergency_strategy` currently engraves Elbereth only at very low HP (< max/5 or < 5) as a last resort. What if I make it engrave at a higher threshold, like < max/3?\n\nElbereth is extremely powerful - it causes most monsters to refuse to approach. If the bot engraves Elbereth earlier, it might survive more early-game encounters.\n\nLet me look at the engraving code in `emergency_strategy` (lines 1643-1651):\n```python\nif self.inventory.engraving_below_me.lower() != 'elbereth' and self.can_engrave() and \\\n        (self.blstats.hitpoints < 1 / 5 * self.blstats.max_hitpoints or self.blstats.hitpoints < 5):\n    yield True\n    self.engrave('Elbereth')\n    for _ in range(8):\n        if self.inventory.engraving_below_me.lower() != 'elbereth':\n            break\n        self.direction('.')\n    return\n```\n\nThis only engraves when HP is very low. What if I change the threshold to HP < max/3?\n\nBut I need to be careful - engraving takes turns, and if the bot is in a combat situation, those turns could be fatal.\n\nActually, let me try a different approach. What if I make the bot engrave Elbereth at the start of the game, or when it first enters a new level? This would give immediate protection.\n\nBut the bot needs to be standing on dust to engrave, and the starting position might not be on dust. Also, the `can_engrave` check ensures the bot isn't already at the forbidden position.\n\nLet me look at when the bot first has the opportunity to engrave. In the `main` function (agent.py line 2039), after the initial setup, the main loop calls `self.global_logic.global_strategy().run()` each turn. The strategies in the chain will eventually offer the engraving opportunity.\n\nActually, let me just try modifying the Elbereth engraving threshold in `emergency_strategy`. Changing from HP < max/5 to HP < max/3 might help more survivors without too much risk.","time":{"start":1790541315650,"end":1790541336999}}}
